# THE COMPOSED REMAINDER, PIVOTED THROUGH THE PARTS, BESIDE THE ONE layers/remainder.sqlc
# DERIVES FROM THE COMPOSED TOTALS. One row per composed layer that owes an exact remainder.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ `r_composed = F Phi r_parts - e_n + e_d`, AND THE POINT IS THAT IT IS NOT
#    `n_composed - d_composed`. One conversion factor multiplies BOTH a part's nameplate and its
#    demand, so the two converted totals are correlated and differencing them counts that
#    factor's spread twice. Converting each part's OWN remainder puts one occurrence of the
#    factor on each term and the correlation is gone. composition/settled_remainders.sqlc is
#    that pivot, one row per settled node with the path's factors already multiplied in; this is
#    the sum over it plus the correction.
#
# ⛔⛔ BOTH ELIMINATIONS APPEAR AND THEIR SIGNS ARE OPPOSITE. Removing double-counted DEMAND
#    raises the remainder; removing double-counted NAMEPLATE lowers it. ⛔ An identity written in
#    prose and evaluated nowhere can lose a whole term without anything noticing, and this one
#    is the identity `docs/linear-algebra.md` states. This relation is what evaluates it.
#
# ⛔⛔⛔ AN ELIMINATION ENTERS AT THE SAME END OF THE SHARED DRIVER AS THE `Σ φ r` TERM IT
#    CORRECTS, AND WHICH END THAT IS DEPENDS ON WHICH DRIVER IS SHARED. `e` is a quantity
#    counted twice, so it moves with the quantity it was counted in, and the corner it takes
#    follows the scenario the sum is read at rather than the sign in front of it.
#
#      no factor spread          the driver is `d`, and `r = n - d` FALLS as `d` rises, so
#                                `r_low` sits at `d`'s high and takes `e_d`'s HIGH
#      spread factor, r > 0      both `n` and `d` scale with the factor, so `r` RISES with it,
#                                and `r_low` sits at the factor's low end and takes `e_d`'s LOW
#
#    ⭐ `e_n` IS NEVER REVERSED. `n` is not the crossed operand in `r = n - d`, so a nameplate
#    elimination enters low with low under either driver. Only `e_d` flips.
#
# ⛔⛔ THE SIGN OF THE SUM IS NOT THE DISCRIMINATOR, IT ONLY LOOKS LIKE ONE. It agrees with the
#    driver test on every layer whose `r` keeps one sign, and disagrees where `r` STRADDLES
#    zero, because there the sign changes between the two corners while the driver does not.
#    `every-local-part/both-views` is that layer: two identical parts and eliminations equal to
#    one whole copy of each, so removing one copy from two must leave exactly one copy. Read
#    with the sign test its high comes out 600 too large, which is `e_d`'s own width.
#
#    ⭐ The three layers that can tell these rules apart, and the figure each must land on:
#      merge-group-composition/labour        driver d, r < 0        (-3.0, -1.5, -0.4)
#      merge-holding-composition/compute     driver phi, r > 0      (1414.0, 2857.0, 4085.6)
#      every-local-part/both-views           driver d, r straddles  (-240, 160, 360)
#
# ⛔ THE `coalesce(..., 0)` IS SOUND ONLY BECAUSE composition/owed_remainder.sqlc IS INNER
#   JOINED FIRST. Inside that population an absent elimination means the composer looked and
#   found no double counting, which is the number zero. Outside it the same absence means nobody
#   looked, and filling it would put a claim in the checker the document declined to make.
#
# ⚠️⚠️ AND THE IDENTITY NEVER NAMES THE CORNER `e` TAKES, SO A READER HAS TO DERIVE IT.
#    `x_composed = F Phi x - e` is silent on it, and the corpus is not: composition/fused.sqlc
#    composes a demand component-wise as `sum(d_high) - e_d_high`, and every filed composed
#    demand reproduces exactly under that rule. Only an elimination with WIDTH can tell the two
#    rules apart, and they are few enough to list rather than count here:
#
#      SELECT composition, composed_layer, quantity
#      FROM ( eliminations/filed ) WHERE high > low
#
#    Every one is a demand and every one composes component-wise. A point elimination has no
#    corners to choose between, so the rest of the corpus is silent by construction.
#
#    ⛔ THE CROSSING FOLLOWS THE OPERAND AND NOT THE SIGN IN FRONT OF IT. `r_low` pairs `n_low`
#    with `d_HIGH`, and the composed demand at its high is `sum(d_high) - e_d_high`, so the
#    elimination reaching `r_low` is `e_d` at ITS high. `e_n` is read straight, because `n` is
#    not crossed in `r`.
#
#    ⚠️ WHERE A FILED REMAINDER ADDS `e_d` STRAIGHT IT SITS ONE ELIMINATION-WIDTH BELOW THIS
#    FIGURE, which is the right rule for an addition applied to an operand that crosses.
#    Observation 13 prints this relation beside the differenced one. Two figures in one document
#    cannot both be right where an elimination has spread, and which of them moves is a filer's
#    call rather than this relation's.
#
# ⚠️ IT TREATS `e_n` AND `e_d` AS INDEPENDENT OF EACH OTHER AND OF THE PARTS, WHICH IS AN
#   ASSUMPTION AND NOT A DERIVATION. They are separately filed observations about different
#   quantities, so nothing here says they move together. If a filer ever states that one double
#   count generates both, this sum decorrelates them the way the composed totals decorrelate
#   `phi`, and `pm:Coupling` is the element that would say so.
-- composition/settled_remainders.sqlc summed over the settled frontier, less eliminations/filed.sqlc.
SELECT c.composition, c.composed_layer,
       sum(c.r_low)  - coalesce(max(en.low),  0) - coalesce(max(cn.e_low),  0)
         + CASE WHEN bool_or(c.spread_factor) AND sum(c.r_low) >= 0
                THEN coalesce(max(ed.low),  0) + coalesce(max(cd.e_low),  0)
                ELSE coalesce(max(ed.high), 0) + coalesce(max(cd.e_high), 0) END AS pivoted_low,
       sum(c.r_mode) - coalesce(max(en.mode), 0) - coalesce(max(cn.e_mode), 0)
                     + coalesce(max(ed.mode), 0) + coalesce(max(cd.e_mode), 0)   AS pivoted_mode,
       sum(c.r_high) - coalesce(max(en.high), 0) - coalesce(max(cn.e_high), 0)
         + CASE WHEN bool_or(c.spread_factor) AND sum(c.r_low) >= 0
                THEN coalesce(max(ed.high), 0) + coalesce(max(cd.e_high), 0)
                ELSE coalesce(max(ed.low),  0) + coalesce(max(cd.e_low),  0) END AS pivoted_high,
       max(d.r_low)  AS derived_low,
       max(d.r_mode) AS derived_mode,
       max(d.r_high) AS derived_high,
       max(d.derived_fit) AS derived_fit,
       max(d.unit)   AS unit,
       count(*)      AS parts
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/settled_remainders.sqlc)
) c
JOIN      (
    :compose(composition/owed_remainder.sqlc)
) o  ON o.filing = c.composition AND o.layer = c.composed_layer
JOIN      (
    :compose(layers/differenced_remainder.sqlc)
) d  ON d.filing = c.composition AND d.layer = c.composed_layer
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(eliminations/filed.sqlc)
) en ON en.composition = c.composition AND en.composed_layer = c.composed_layer
    AND en.quantity = 'nameplate'
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(eliminations/filed.sqlc)
) ed ON ed.composition = c.composition AND ed.composed_layer = c.composed_layer
    AND ed.quantity = 'demand'
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(composition/carried_eliminations.sqlc)
) cn ON cn.composition = c.composition AND cn.composed_layer = c.composed_layer
    AND cn.quantity = 'nameplate'
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(composition/carried_eliminations.sqlc)
) cd ON cd.composition = c.composition AND cd.composed_layer = c.composed_layer
    AND cd.quantity = 'demand'
GROUP BY c.composition, c.composed_layer
